      "heading": "Title",
      "chunks": [
        {
          "chunk_index": 1,
          "chunk_id": "s01_c01",
          "anchor_id": "cue_s01_c1_neupl_neural_population_learning_pub",
          "text": "\"This is NeuPL, or Neural Population Learning, published at ICLR 2022 by researchers at University College London and DeepMind.",
          "expected_role": "content",
          "cue_keywords": [
            "neupl",
            "neural",
            "population",
            "learning",
            "published",
            "iclr",
            "2022",
            "researchers",
            "university",
            "college"
          ],
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "chunk_index": 2,
          "chunk_id": "s01_c02",
          "anchor_id": "cue_s01_c2_learning_play_strategy_games_like",
          "text": "Learning to play strategy games like StarCraft or poker requires discovering a whole population of diverse policies, not just one.",
          "expected_role": "method",
          "cue_keywords": [
            "learning",
            "play",
            "strategy",
            "games",
            "like",
            "starcraft",
            "poker",
            "requires",
            "discovering",
            "whole"
          ],
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "chunk_index": 3,
          "chunk_id": "s01_c03",
          "anchor_id": "cue_s01_c3_usual_recipe_grows_population_iterat",
          "text": "The usual recipe grows this population by iteratively training a fresh policy to beat the previous ones, but in real games that approach wastes compute relearning basic skills at every step and produces under-trained policies when budgets run out.",
          "expected_role": "method",
          "cue_keywords": [
            "usual",
            "recipe",
            "grows",
            "population",
            "iteratively",
            "fresh",
            "policy",
            "beat",
            "previous",
            "ones"
          ],
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "chunk_index": 4,
          "chunk_id": "s01_c04",
          "anchor_id": "cue_s01_c4_neupl_solves_both_problems_represent",
          "text": "NeuPL solves both problems by representing the entire population inside a single conditional neural network, so skills transfer freely across policies while still enjoying convergence guarantees.\"",
          "expected_role": "content",
          "cue_keywords": [
            "neupl",
            "solves",
            "both",
            "problems",
            "representing",
            "entire",
            "population",
            "inside",
            "single",
            "conditional"
          ],
          "required": true
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "index": 2,
      "id": "problem",
      "heading": "Problem",
      "chunks": [
        {
          "chunk_index": 1,
          "chunk_id": "s02_c01",
          "anchor_id": "cue_s02_c1_classical_population_learning_method",
          "text": "\"Classical population-learning methods such as Policy Space Response Oracles grow a set of strategies by repeatedly training a new policy to best-respond to a mixture over the existing ones.",
          "expected_role": "method",
          "cue_keywords": [
            "classical",
            "population-learning",
            "methods",
            "such",
            "policy",
            "space",
            "response",
            "oracles",
            "grow",
            "set"
          ],
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "chunk_index": 2,
          "chunk_id": "s02_c02",
          "anchor_id": "cue_s02_c2_toy_normal_form_games_works_cleanly",
          "text": "In toy normal-form games this works cleanly because best-responses can be solved exactly.",
          "expected_role": "content",
          "cue_keywords": [
            "toy",
            "normal-form",
            "games",
            "works",
            "cleanly",
            "because",
            "best-responses",
            "solved",
            "exactly"
          ],
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "chunk_index": 3,
          "chunk_id": "s02_c03",
          "anchor_id": "cue_s02_c3_but_real_world_games_temporal_partia",
          "text": "But real-world games are temporal and partially observed, so best-responses can only be approximated with expensive deep reinforcement learning.",
          "expected_role": "content",
          "cue_keywords": [
            "but",
            "real-world",
            "games",
            "temporal",
            "partially",
            "observed",
            "best-responses",
            "only",
            "approximated",
            "expensive"
          ],
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "chunk_index": 4,
          "chunk_id": "s02_c04",
          "anchor_id": "cue_s02_c4_creates_two_failures_under_finite",
          "text": "This creates two failures: under a finite budget you cannot tell a truly converged best-response from one stuck at a local plateau, so iterations get truncated prematurely and pollute the population with weak policies; and every new policy relearns basic skills from scratch, which becomes intractable as opponents grow stronger.\"",
          "expected_role": "method",
          "cue_keywords": [
            "creates",
            "two",
            "failures",
            "under",
            "finite",
            "budget",
            "you",
            "cannot",
            "tell",
            "truly"
          ],
          "required": true
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "index": 3,
      "id": "motivation",
      "heading": "Motivation",
      "chunks": [
        {
          "chunk_index": 1,
          "chunk_id": "s03_c01",
          "anchor_id": "cue_s03_c1_need_population_strategies_rooted_ga",
          "text": "\"The need for a population of strategies is rooted in game theory: in a purely cyclic game like rock-paper-scissors a single strategy is meaningless, since improving against one opponent means losing to another.",
          "expected_role": "method",
          "cue_keywords": [
            "need",
            "population",
            "strategies",
            "rooted",
            "game",
            "theory",
            "purely",
            "cyclic",
            "game",
            "like"
          ],
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "chunk_index": 2,
          "chunk_id": "s03_c02",
          "anchor_id": "cue_s03_c2_prior_frameworks_handle_policies_one",
          "text": "Prior frameworks handle this by training policies one at a time and discarding the shared knowledge between them.",
          "expected_role": "method",
          "cue_keywords": [
            "prior",
            "frameworks",
            "handle",
            "policies",
            "one",
            "time",
            "discarding",
            "shared",
            "knowledge",
            "between"
          ],
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "chunk_index": 3,
          "chunk_id": "s03_c03",
          "anchor_id": "cue_s03_c3_neupl_insight_these_policies_overlap",
          "text": "NeuPL's insight is that these policies overlap enormously — they share perception, memory, and motor skills — so training them independently is wasteful.",
          "expected_role": "method",
          "cue_keywords": [
            "neupl",
            "insight",
            "these",
            "policies",
            "overlap",
            "enormously",
            "they",
            "share",
            "perception",
            "memory"
          ],
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "chunk_index": 4,
          "chunk_id": "s03_c04",
          "anchor_id": "cue_s03_c4_single_could_hold_whole_population",
          "text": "If a single model could hold the whole population and condition its behaviour on which opponents it faces, early skills learned against weak opponents would directly bootstrap the discovery of exploiters to much stronger ones.\"",
          "expected_role": "method",
          "cue_keywords": [
            "single",
            "could",
            "hold",
            "whole",
            "population",
            "condition",
            "its",
            "behaviour",
            "which",
            "opponents"
          ],
          "required": true
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "index": 4,
      "id": "contribution",
      "heading": "Contribution",
      "chunks": [
        {
          "chunk_index": 1,
          "chunk_id": "s04_c01",
          "anchor_id": "cue_s04_c1_neupl_makes_three_contributions",
          "text": "\"NeuPL makes three contributions.",
          "expected_role": "content",
          "cue_keywords": [
            "neupl",
            "makes",
            "three",
            "contributions"
          ],
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "chunk_index": 2,
          "chunk_id": "s04_c02",
          "anchor_id": "cue_s04_c2_first_single_conditional_represents",
          "text": "First, it is a single conditional model that represents a whole population of policies, conditioned on a meta-game mixture strategy that specifies which opponents each policy should beat.",
          "expected_role": "method",
          "cue_keywords": [
            "first",
            "single",
            "conditional",
            "represents",
            "whole",
            "population",
            "policies",
            "conditioned",
            "meta-game",
            "mixture"
          ],
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "chunk_index": 3,
          "chunk_id": "s04_c03",
          "anchor_id": "cue_s04_c3_second_unifying_framework_choosing_i",
          "text": "Second, it is a unifying framework: by choosing the interaction graph you recover self-play, fictitious play, or PSRO-Nash as special cases, and you can even express cyclic graphs that PSRO cannot.",
          "expected_role": "guidance",
          "cue_keywords": [
            "second",
            "unifying",
            "framework",
            "choosing",
            "interaction",
            "graph",
            "you",
            "recover",
            "self-play",
            "fictitious"
          ],
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "chunk_index": 4,
          "chunk_id": "s04_c04",
          "anchor_id": "cue_s04_c4_third_comes_convergence_guarantees_g",
          "text": "Third, it comes with convergence guarantees — for grounded, lower-triangular interaction graphs and a suitable meta-graph solver, NeuPL provably converges to an N-step best-response and, with large enough N, to a normal-form Nash equilibrium.\"",
          "expected_role": "content",
          "cue_keywords": [
            "third",
            "comes",
            "convergence",
            "guarantees",
            "grounded",
            "lower-triangular",
            "interaction",
            "graphs",
            "suitable",
            "meta-graph"
          ],
          "required": true
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "index": 5,
      "id": "method",
      "heading": "Method",
      "chunks": [
        {
          "chunk_index": 1,
          "chunk_id": "s05_c01",
          "anchor_id": "cue_s05_c1_concretely_neupl_trains_one_opponent",
          "text": "\"Concretely, NeuPL trains one opponent-conditioned network. Each policy index i is paired with a meta-strategy vector sigma-i that says, as a probability distribution, which other policies it should play against.",
          "expected_role": "method",
          "cue_keywords": [
            "concretely",
            "neupl",
            "trains",
            "one",
            "opponent-conditioned",
            "network",
            "policy",
            "index",
            "paired",
            "meta-strategy"
          ],
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "chunk_index": 2,
          "chunk_id": "s05_c02",
          "anchor_id": "cue_s05_c2_network_maximizes_discounted_return",
          "text": "The network maximizes discounted return under a double expectation — first over the sampled opponent, then over the game dynamics induced by both policies.",
          "expected_role": "content",
          "cue_keywords": [
            "network",
            "maximizes",
            "discounted",
            "return",
            "under",
            "double",
            "expectation",
            "first",
            "over",
            "sampled"
          ],
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "chunk_index": 3,
          "chunk_id": "s05_c03",
          "anchor_id": "cue_s05_c3_optimize_reinforcement_learning_neup",
          "text": "To optimize this by reinforcement learning, NeuPL jointly trains an opponent-conditioned action-value function, and additionally learns a payoff estimator that predicts the expected outcome of any policy pairing directly from the value function, so the full payoff matrix can be evaluated cheaply without replaying every matchup.",
          "expected_role": "content",
          "cue_keywords": [
            "optimize",
            "reinforcement",
            "learning",
            "neupl",
            "jointly",
            "trains",
            "opponent-conditioned",
            "action-value",
            "function",
            "additionally"
          ],
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "chunk_index": 4,
          "chunk_id": "s05_c04",
          "anchor_id": "cue_s05_c4_interaction_graph_assigns_opponents",
          "text": "The interaction graph that assigns opponents can be a fixed matrix — reproducing fictitious play — or adaptive, recomputed from the learned payoffs by a meta-graph solver, reproducing PSRO-Nash. Because the graph, not a handcrafted schedule, controls which objectives are active, the number of genuinely distinct policies grows on its own as the game demands.\"",
          "expected_role": "content",
          "cue_keywords": [
            "interaction",
            "graph",
            "assigns",
            "opponents",
            "fixed",
            "matrix",
            "reproducing",
            "fictitious",
            "play",
            "adaptive"
          ],
          "required": true
        }
      ]
    },
    {
